<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kara Koyunlu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kara Koyunlu, or Black Sheep Turkomans, were a Turkoman tribal confederation that ruled parts of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. They were rivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>. They absorbed western Persian territories as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> fragmented.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu.jpg" alt="Kara Koyunlu" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Demis Map Server · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Timurid_and_Qara_Qoyunlu_domains,_1458.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Qara Qoyunlu, or Black Sheep Turkomans, were a Turkoman, culturally Persianate, Muslim dynasty that ruled from about 1374 to 1468. Their name means those with black sheep, probably from a black sheep painted on their flags. The ruling family descended from the Baharlu branch of the Yıwa tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, which held territory north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> by the fourteenth century. Other tribes of the confederation included the Saadlu, the Karamanlu, the Dukharlu and the Jagirlu. The Oghuz dialect they spoke is connected by a number of researchers with the Azerbaijani language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Qara Qoyunlu became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirid Sultanate</a> from about 1375. In 1400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> defeated them, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Released after Timur's death in 1405, he defeated the Timurid Abu Bakr at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> on 14 October 1406 and, on 20 April 1408, Miran Shah at Sardrud. The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and the execution of Shaykh Uways Jalayir in 1410 secured independence, and in the same year the Qara Qoyunlu took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. After Qara Yusuf died in 1420 the Timurid Shah Rukh defeated his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-iskander">Qara Iskander</a>, and in 1436 made Jahan Shah, Iskander's brother, his vassal.</p><p>Jahan Shah was crowned on 19 April 1438 and became independent after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a>'s death in 1447. He took Sultaniya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and Qazvin, and in 1452–1453 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a>; in 1458 he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> for some months. His reign was troubled by rebellious sons. In 1466 his attempt to take Diyarbakır from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> failed, and he died in the Battle of Chapakchur in 1467. By 1468 the Aq Qoyunlu under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> had defeated the Qara Qoyunlu and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Armenian sources record heavy taxes, devastation and captivity under Iskander and Jahan Shah, though Jahan Shah approved the move of the Catholicos's seat to Etchmiadzin in 1441.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The state's organisation followed that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanids</a>, and Persian was the language of diplomacy, poetry and court. Jahan Shah wrote poetry in Azerbaijani and Persian. His buildings include the northern iwan of the Darb-e Imam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, from 1453, and the Blue Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, completed in 1465, whose tilework may have influenced the later blue-and-white tiles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and Ottoman buildings. His son Pir Budaq, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, was active in the making of refined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk">Sultan Quli</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahi dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, and Bairam Khan of the Mughal service belonged to the Qara Qoyunlu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qara Qoyunlu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Osman fought the Anatolian ruler Kadi Burhan al-Din, whom he defeated and killed in 1398, and the rival Qara Qoyunlu, the ‘<b>Black Sheep</b>’ Turkmens of Azerbaijan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Oudh came from a Shia Muslim Sayyid family of Nishapur in Persia, descended from Musa al-Kadhim, and also traced descent on the paternal line to the <b>Kara Koyunlu</b> through Qara Yusuf; they were known for their secularism and broad outlook, and promoted Shia as the state religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander I the Great (r. 1412–1442) rebuilt churches and fortifications, but the <b>Kara Koyunlu</b> ruler Jahan Shah sacked Tbilisi in 1440, and the fall of Constantinople in 1453 cut Georgia off from Christian Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the following decades the Turkmen confederations of the <b>Kara Koyunlu</b> and then the Aq Qoyunlu absorbed the western Persian territories, and Abu Sa'id (r. 1451–1469), the last Timurid to reunite much of the realm, was defeated and killed in 1469 while campaigning against the Aq Qoyunlu leader…</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
